    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "Thanks for tagging me @aakhundov! yeah we discussed an idea for a similar feature although maybe with a slightly different use case originally.\r\n\r\nI think the idea that we discussed was that if a user provided the equivalent PyTorch code for a user-defined Triton kernel it would allow inductor to potentially fuse the user-defined Triton kernel with some other operator (or as mentioned replace it with the PyTorch ops while autotuning). This was in the context of the \"user-defined + inductor-generated Triton kernel fusion\"-line of work.\r\n\r\nThe main downside of this proposal IIRC was that the equivalent PyTorch code may not be expressed in a way that would lead to efficient codegen when compared to the user-defined Triton kernel I think? There is a potential issue that in theory users could provide \"equivalent\" PyTorch code that isn't actually equivalent to the user-defined kernel which could lead to silent incorrectness or hard to identify bugs based on autotuning results/different fused operators. @aakhundov might recall the negatives a bit better here.\r\n\r\nIf there is interest we can explore writing up a design doc for this",
